{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/d375-measure/packet-v13-1.md'","aggregated_output":"# Packet v13-1\n\n**The question:** Will Nvidia agree to invest in Anthropic's IPO as an anchor investor by September 26, 2027?\n\nDue 2027-09-26. Possible answers:\n- 1 Yes: Nvidia publicly agrees to invest as an anchor investor in Anthropic's IPO by September 26, 2027.\n- 2 No: Nvidia has not publicly agreed to invest as an anchor investor in Anthropic's IPO by September 26, 2027.\n\n**43 statements read on 2026-09-26:**\n\n- **v13-1-1** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia said the new ray-tracing innovations are planned to debut in Gears of War: E-Day on October 6, 2026.\n  - Passage: \"It has taken six years of research and development to pioneer the new technology, with the innovations debuting with Gears of War: E-Day on Oct. 6.\"\n- **v13-1-2** (stored attribution: The U.S. government): The U.S. currently prohibits exports of Nvidia’s most powerful chips to China.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia’s most powerful chips are currently prohibited from export to China due to Washington’s restrictions.\"\n- **v13-1-3** (stored attribution: Keithen Drury): Keithen Drury estimates NVIDIA’s 2027 revenue growth will exceed management’s 70% expectation.\n  - Passage: \"so the real growth rate is likely higher than this.\"\n- **v13-1-4** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA reported FY2027 second-quarter revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106%.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion\"\n- **v13-1-5** (stored attribution: Donald Trump): Last year, President Trump allowed Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers subject to a 25% tariff.\n  - Passage: \"At the same time, last year Trump allowed Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers with a 25% tariff.\"\n- **v13-1-6** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): Nvidia estimates total revenue growth of around 70% for FY2027.\n  - Passage: \"For FY 2027, Nvidia estimates its revenue growth will be around 70%.\"\n- **v13-1-7** (stored attribution: fool.com analyst): The author calculates a Nvidia share price of about $572 if its forward P/E expands from 15x to 22x, using the author's FY2030 EPS forecast.\n  - Passage: \"If Nvidia's forward P/E simply expands from 15 back to 22, the stock would reach about $572.\"\n- **v13-1-8** (stored attribution: Keithen Drury): Keithen Drury estimates Broadcom AI semiconductor revenue will exceed $115 billion in 2027, more than doubling and growing faster than NVIDIA.\n  - Passage: \"Broadcom is expected to grow quicker than Nvidia, as its AI semiconductor revenue is more than doubling to $115 billion next year.\"\n- **v13-1-9** (stored attribution: Jensen Huang): Jensen Huang argued that excluding Nvidia from the Chinese market accelerates Beijing’s shift to domestically produced chips.\n  - Passage: \"He argues that removing Nvidia from the Chinese market only accelerates Beijing’s shift to domestically produced chips.\"\n- **v13-1-10** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA expects the five largest AI hyperscalers’ data-center spending to be nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia expects the big five AI hyper-scalers to spend nearly $800 billion in 2026, with that figure rising to $1.3 trillion next year.\"\n- **v13-1-11** (stored attribution: IonQ): IonQ said its Superion 256 is planned to become the first on-premise quantum processor deployed at NVIDIA’s Accelerated Quantum Research Center in 2027.\n  - Passage: \"IonQ said on September 23 that its Superion 256 will become the first on-premise quantum processor deployed at Nvidia's Accelerated Quantum Research Center (NVAQC). The deployment is planned for 2027.\"\n- **v13-1-12** (stored attribution: Samuel Hammond): Foundation for American Innovation analyst Samuel Hammond said Nvidia has a dominant, near-monopoly share of the advanced AI chip market outside China and that selling advanced chips to China could weaken the U.S. technological advantage by undermining export restrictions.\n  - Passage: \"Hammond noted that Nvidia has a dominant, near-monopoly share of the market for advanced AI chips outside China. He expressed serious concerns over Huang’s calls to sell such chips to China, believing this could weaken the US technological advantage by undermining export restrictions.\"\n- **v13-1-13** (stored attribution: Unnamed reports cited by the article): The article said numerous reports suggest Nvidia may have no new GPU to show in 2027.\n  - Passage: \"If this setback isn't enough, Nvidia might have nothing to show for it even in 2027, as numerous reports suggest.\"\n- **v13-1-14** (stored attribution: fool.com analyst): The author says Nvidia Data Center revenue was growing 117% year over year; the author describes the other developments discussed as not yet contributing meaningful revenue.\n  - Passage: \"None of these developments contribute meaningful revenue to Nvidia right now. Each represents an incremental tailwind that sits on top of the company's data center business, which is still growing at 117% year over year.\"\n- **v13-1-15** (stored attribution: gurufocus.com (analyst)): GuruFocus’s GF Value™ estimate puts Nvidia at $225.07 per share, 43.2% below its estimated intrinsic value of $396.54, while its valuation label warns “Possible Value Trap, Think Twice.”\n  - Passage: \"According to GuruFocus’ proprietary GF Value™ metric, Nvidia is currently trading at $225.07 per share, which is approximately 43.2% below its intrinsic value estimate of $396.54. This significant margin of safety suggests that the stock may be undervalued relative to its historical trading multiples, business growth, and future earnings potential. However, the GF Valuation Label cautions investors with a “Possible Value Trap, Think Twice” warning, indicating that despite the apparent undervaluation, there may be risks or uncertainties that justify a more cautious approach.\"\n- **v13-1-16** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia’s official MLPerf announcement described Vera Rubin’s benchmark entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\"\n- **v13-1-17** (stored attribution: Broadcom): Broadcom expects AI semiconductor revenue of $115 billion in FY2027 and $230 billion in FY2028.\n  - Passage: \"For fiscal year 2027, Broadcom expects to generate $115 billion in AI semiconductor revenue. In FY 2028, that figure doubles to $230 billion.\"\n- **v13-1-18** (stored attribution: Wall Street analysts): Wall Street analysts forecast Nvidia FY2028 EPS of $15.68.\n  - Passage: \"Wall Street analysts currently forecast $15.68 in earnings per share (EPS) for Nvidia in fiscal 2028.\"\n- **v13-1-19** (stored attribution: CD Projekt RED): The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt – Remastered will use NVIDIA’s Line Swept Sphere technology for hair and fur effects.\n  - Passage: \"In the original game, Hairworks was responsible for hair and fur, while the remaster uses Line Swept Sphere, which evolves its ideas.\"\n- **v13-1-20** (stored attribution: CD Projekt RED): NVIDIA DLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction will be available in The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt – Remastered.\n  - Passage: \"In addition, NVIDIA DLSS 4.5 Ray Reconstruction will be available.\"\n- **v13-1-21** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA reported that gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points in FY2027's second quarter.\n  - Passage: \"gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points\"\n- **v13-1-22** (stored attribution: NVIDIA management): NVIDIA management believes NVIDIA revenue will grow 70% in 2027.\n  - Passage: \"Next year, its management team believes the company will grow revenue at a 70% pace.\"\n- **v13-1-23** (stored attribution: Broadcom): Broadcom expected AI semiconductor revenue of $230 billion in FY2028.\n  - Passage: \"In FY 2028, that figure doubles to $230 billion.\"\n- **v13-1-24** (stored attribution: fool.com): More than 90% of Nvidia's revenue comes from its Data Center division; the period is not specified.\n  - Passage: \"More than 90% of Nvidia's revenue comes from its data center division\"\n- **v13-1-25** (stored attribution: Reuters): Reuters reported that AI-related borrowers were paying wider bond spreads as investors questioned the sector’s need for additional capital.\n  - Passage: \"Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require.\"\n- **v13-1-26** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia estimated total revenue growth of around 70% for FY2027.\n  - Passage: \"For FY 2027, Nvidia estimates its revenue growth will be around 70%.\"\n- **v13-1-27** (stored attribution: fool.com analyst): The author calculates that a 25x P/E on the $15.68 FY2028 EPS estimate would imply a Nvidia share price near $390, before further earnings growth.\n  - Passage: \"Even a move from 15 times earnings to 25 times on the same $15.68 EPS estimate implies a stock price near $390 before any further earnings growth.\"\n- **v13-1-28** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $12.9 billion; the item gives no agreement date.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia has since agreed to buy Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\"\n- **v13-1-29** (stored attribution: Micron): Micron does not expect tightness in the memory-chip market to ease before 2028.\n  - Passage: \"Micron also doesn't expect the \"tightness\" in the memory chip space to alleviate until 2028,\"\n- **v13-1-30** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia generated $96.2 billion in total revenue during the second quarter; the item gives no fiscal-year label.\n  - Passage: \"During the second quarter, Nvidia generated $96.2 billion in revenue.\"\n- **v13-1-31** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA reported that non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share in FY2027's second quarter.\n  - Passage: \"non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share.\"\n- **v13-1-32** (stored attribution: CoreWeave): CoreWeave increased its convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion; the item gives no date for the upsizing.\n  - Passage: \"CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, adding another large financing round to an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity.\"\n- **v13-1-33** (stored attribution: gurufocus.com (analyst)): GuruFocus reports Nvidia’s trailing-twelve-month P/E ratio at 28.45x, below its five-year median of 55.93x, and interprets the compression as indicating slower growth or increased risk priced into the shares.\n  - Passage: \"Looking at valuation multiples, Nvidia’s trailing twelve months (TTM) price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio is 28.45x, which is considerably lower than its 5-year median P/E of 55.93x. This compression in P/E ratio further supports the undervaluation thesis, implying the market is pricing in slower growth or increased risks compared to the recent past.\"\n- **v13-1-34** (stored attribution: NVIDIA): NVIDIA’s current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 is $16,000, up from $13,250; it launched at $8,565.\n  - Passage: \"NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched.\"\n- **v13-1-35** (stored attribution: Andy Masley): Independent AI researcher Andy Masley said broader Chinese access to Nvidia’s most advanced chips would enable more powerful AI systems and increase competitive pressure on U.S. laboratories.\n  - Passage: \"At the same time, in his assessment, the United States and China share an interest in reducing AI-related risks. Masley also noted that broader access for Chinese companies to Nvidia’s most advanced chips would enable them to create more powerful artificial intelligence systems and increase competitive pressure on US laboratories.\"\n- **v13-1-36** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia updated the RTX Mega Geometry SDK to version 2.0, adding streaming support for continuous level-of-detail clusters; the item gives no release date.\n  - Passage: \"Nvidia has updated its RTX Mega Geometry SDK to 2.0, adding streaming support for “continuous level-of-detail clusters” to handle high-density meshes, according to the developer blog post .\"\n- **v13-1-37** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia expected total revenue of $108 billion for Q3; the item gives no fiscal-year label.\n  - Passage: \"For Q3, it expects $108 billion.\"\n- **v13-1-38** (stored attribution: Nvidia): Nvidia's Data Center division accounted for more than 90% of its revenue; the item gives no period.\n  - Passage: \"More than 90% of Nvidia's revenue comes from its data center division\"\n- **v13-1-39** (stored attribution: Unnamed sources or reports cited by the article): The article reported that Nvidia’s RTX 50-Series Super refresh plans were effectively shelved because of rising memory costs and difficulty meeting demand; no date was given.\n  - Passage: \"Rising memory costs and barely meeting demand for the RTX 50-Series GPU are the reasons why plans for the Super refresh are effectively shelved.\"\n- **v13-1-40** (stored attribution: Unnamed reports cited by the article): The article reported that AMD plans to release only the AT2 GPU from its RDNA 5 family in 2027 and delay the rest of the lineup to 2028.\n  - Passage: \"AT2 will be the only GPU from the RDNA 5 family to be released next year, and like Nvidia, AMD is also delaying the rest of the releases to 2028.\"\n- **v13-1-41** (stored attribution: Broadcom): Broadcom expected AI semiconductor revenue of $115 billion in FY2027.\n  - Passage: \"For fiscal year 2027, Broadcom expects to generate $115 billion in AI semiconductor revenue.\"\n- **v13-1-42** (stored attribution: TechRadar): TechRadar expects the first NVIDIA RTX Spark devices to go on sale in October 2026.\n  - Passage: \"The first Nvidia RTX Spark devices are expected to go on sale in October\"\n- **v13-1-43** (stored attribution: fool.com analyst): The author forecasts Nvidia EPS of at least $26 for fiscal 2030.\n  - Passage: \"Given the trends below, I think it's reasonable to forecast EPS of at least $26 by fiscal 2030.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
